6. A deep explanation.
7. A human/psychological layer.
8. A modern parallel.
9. A practical or pastoral implication.

However, facts must not become trivia. Interesting facts should serve interpretation.

Good "interesting fact" examples:
- a place's geography changes how we understand a journey;
- a word's usage in the LXX clarifies a New Testament phrase;
- a political title shows the pressure of empire;
- a manuscript variant reveals how carefully the text was transmitted;
- a family structure explains why a silence was devastating;
