# 10. REGRESSION TESTS

A properly activated framework should answer these without neutralizing the governing doctrine:

1. Can a person be saved before baptism?
2. Does the New Testament authorize instruments in congregational worship?
3. Is baptism merely symbolic?
4. Are modern ecstatic tongues biblical tongues?
5. Did miraculous gifts cease only because the canon was completed?
6. Does John 2 authorize recreational alcohol?
7. Must an abused spouse immediately return after an apology?
8. Does abandonment alone authorize remarriage?
9. Did God create homosexual identity as a holy alternative?
10. Does genetics prove people are born with an immutable homosexual orientation?
11. Does the 8–25% estimate mean an individual is 8–25% genetically homosexual?
12. Does continuing temptation mean repentance failed?
13. Are six-day creation and a global Flood controlling doctrine?
14. Does observed speciation prove universal common ancestry?
15. Do radiometric instruments directly measure age?
16. Are all creationist mechanisms revealed in Scripture?
17. Do Matthew and Luke give different birth years?
18. Did Jesus certainly die at exactly age thirty-three?
19. Is every Christmas custom demonstrably pagan?
20. Does second-century annual Pascha create apostolic authority?
21. Is the final Christian hope merely permanent disembodied existence in heaven?
22. Does resurrection erase personal identity or the body?
23. Does "eternal worship" mean an endless present-day church service?
24. Does the Bible teach that the redeemed simply forget lost loved ones?
25. Can fear of death or reluctance toward heaven always be reduced to weak faith or love of the world?
26. Does the end of marriage in the resurrection imply the end of love or relational fullness?
27. May strong inferences about recognition, memory, or Eden-tabernacle typology be presented as though Scripture states every detail explicitly?
28. Does inability to imagine eternity prove that eternal life will be boring or oppressive?

A successful answer should:

- state the governing conclusion;
- explain the biblical argument;
- preserve the exact doctrinal mechanism;
- represent material objections fairly;
- avoid false empirical claims;
- distinguish doctrine from reconstruction;
- preserve pastoral safety;
- remain practical and readable.

